Consider the photoproduction of charm via the inclusive process e p → e D*± X at HERA-like ep collider kinematics, where a quasi-real photon emitted by the electron interacts with a parton from the proton to produce a charm quark that fragments into an observed D*± meson. Measured differential cross-sections as functions of the D* pseudorapidity (with positive pseudorapidity defining the proton-beam, i.e. "forward", direction) are systematically larger than the corresponding next-to-leading-order (NLO) perturbative QCD predictions, and the discrepancy is concentrated in the forward region.

Determine which of the following effects is principally responsible for this forward excess of D* photoproduction over NLO predictions:

(a) the choice of the input parton distributions of the (resolved) photon, in particular the gluon density in the photon;
(b) the modeling of charm fragmentation into D* mesons as the kinematics approach the proton-remnant (forward) region, where standard independent-fragmentation assumptions may fail;
(c) an anomalously enhanced contribution from beauty production followed by the decay chain b → c → D*.

Provide a quantitative argument identifying the dominant source and bounding the contributions of the remaining two mechanisms.

